        "**Problem 1**: 50 g of limestone ($CaCO_3$, 95% pure) is calcined. Calculate the mass of quick lime ($CaO$) produced and the volume of $CO_2$ evolved at STP.\n\n**Solution**:\nMolar mass $CaCO_3 = 100 \\text{ g mol}^{-1}$, $CaO = 56 \\text{ g mol}^{-1}$.\nMass of pure $CaCO_3 = 50 \\times 0.95 = 47.5 \\text{ g}$.\nMoles of $CaCO_3 = 47.5 / 100 = 0.475 \\text{ mol}$.\nFrom stoichiometry: 1 mol $CaCO_3 \\rightarrow$ 1 mol $CaO$ + 1 mol $CO_2$.\nMass of $CaO = 0.475 \\times 56 = 26.6 \\text{ g}$.\nVolume of $CO_2$ at STP = $0.475 \\times 22.4 = 10.64 \\text{ L}$.\n\n**Answer**: $CaO = 26.6 \\text{ g}$; $CO_2 = 10.64 \\text{ L}$ at STP.",
        "**Problem 2**: Calculate the available chlorine (% w/w) in a bleaching powder sample if 2.5 g of it liberates iodine from KI requiring 25 mL of 0.1 M $Na_2S_2O_3$ for titration.\n\n**Solution**:\nReactions: $OCl^- + 2I^- + 2H^+ \\rightarrow I_2 + Cl^- + H_2O$; $I_2 + 2S_2O_3^{2-} \\rightarrow 2I^- + S_4O_6^{2-}$.\n1 mol $OCl^- \\equiv$ 1 mol $I_2 \\equiv$ 2 mol $S_2O_3^{2-}$.\nMoles of $Na_2S_2O_3 = 0.1 \\times 0.025 = 0.0025 \\text{ mol}$.\nMoles of $OCl^-$ (available Cl) = $0.0025 / 2 = 0.00125 \\text{ mol}$.\nMass of $Cl_2$ equivalent = $0.00125 \\times 71 = 0.08875 \\text{ g}$.\n% Available chlorine = $(0.08875 / 2.5) \\times 100 = 3.55%$.\n\n**Answer**: 3.55% available chlorine.",
        "**Problem 3**: 10 g of gypsum ($CaSO_4 \\cdot 2H_2O$) is heated to 130 °C. Calculate the mass of Plaster of Paris obtained and the mass of water lost.\n\n**Solution**:\nMolar mass $CaSO_4 \\cdot 2H_2O = 172 \\text{ g mol}^{-1}$, $CaSO_4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}H_2O = 145 \\text{ g mol}^{-1}$.\nMoles of gypsum = $10 / 172 = 0.05814 \\text{ mol}$.\nMass of Plaster of Paris = $0.05814 \\times 145 = 8.43 \\text{ g}$.\nMass of water lost = $10 - 8.43 = 1.57 \\text{ g}$ (theoretical: $0.05814 \\times 27 = 1.57 \\text{ g}$).\n\n**Answer**: Plaster of Paris = 8.43 g; Water lost = 1.57 g.",
        "**Problem 4**: The solubility product ($K_{sp}$) of $BaSO_4$ is $1.1 \\times 10^{-10}$ at 298 K. Calculate its solubility in mol L$^{-1}$ and g/100 mL. Will a precipitate form if equal volumes of 0.001 M $BaCl_2$ and 0.001 M $Na_2SO_4$ are mixed?\n\n**Solution**:\n$BaSO_4(s) \\rightleftharpoons Ba^{2+} + SO_4^{2-}$; $K_{sp} = s^2$.\n$s = \\sqrt{1.1 \\times 10^{-10}} = 1.05 \\times 10^{-5} \\text{ mol L}^{-1}$.\nMolar mass $BaSO_4 = 233.4 \\text{ g mol}^{-1}$.\nSolubility in g/100 mL = $1.05 \\times 10^{-5} \\times 233.4 \\times 0.1 = 2.45 \\times 10^{-4} \\text{ g/100 mL}$.\nOn mixing equal volumes: $[Ba^{2+}] = [SO_4^{2-}] = 0.0005 \\text{ M}$.\nIonic product = $(5 \\times 10^{-4})^2 = 2.5 \\times 10^{-7} > K_{sp}$.\nSince IP > $K_{sp}$, precipitate forms.\n\n**Answer**: $s = 1.05 \\times 10^{-5} \\text{ M}$ ($2.45 \\times 10^{-4} \\text{ g/100 mL}$); Yes, precipitate forms.",
        "**Problem 5**: Arrange the following in order of increasing thermal stability and explain: $MgCO_3$, $CaCO_3$, $BaCO_3$, $BeCO_3$.\n\n**Solution**:\nOrder: $BeCO_3 < MgCO_3 < CaCO_3 < BaCO_3$ (increasing stability).\nDecomposition temperatures: $BeCO_3$ (unstable at RT) $< MgCO_3$ (350 °C) $< CaCO_3$ (900 °C) $< BaCO_3$ (1360 °C).\nExplanation: Thermal stability depends on polarising power of cation ($M^{2+}$). Smaller cation with high charge density ($Be^{2+}$, $r \\approx 45 \\text{ pm}$) strongly polarises the large $CO_3^{2-}$ anion, weakening $C-O$ bonds and lowering decomposition temperature. As ionic radius increases down the group ($Mg^{2+}$ 72, $Ca^{2+}$ 100, $Ba^{2+}$ 135 pm), polarising power decreases, $CO_3^{2-}$ is less distorted, and stability increases.\n\n**Answer**: $BeCO_3 < MgCO_3 < CaCO_3 < BaCO_3$.",
        "**Problem 6**: A 4.0 g mixture of $MgCO_3$ and $BaCO_3$ on heating gives 0.896 L $CO_2$ at STP. Find the % composition of the mixture.\n\n**Solution**:\nMoles $CO_2 = 0.896 / 22.4 = 0.04 \\text{ mol}$.\nLet $x$ g = $MgCO_3$, $(4-x)$ g = $BaCO_3$.\nMolar masses: $MgCO_3 = 84$, $BaCO_3 = 197$.\n$x/84 + (4-x)/197 = 0.04$.\nMultiplying by $(84 \\times 197) = 16548$: $197x + 84(4-x) = 0.04 \\times 16548 = 661.92$.\n$197x + 336 - 84x = 661.92$.\n$113x = 325.92$.\n$x = 2.884 \\text{ g}$ ($MgCO_3$).\n$(4-x) = 1.116 \\text{ g}$ ($BaCO_3$).\n% $MgCO_3 = (2.884/4) \\times 100 = 72.1%$.\n% $BaCO_3 = (1.116/4) \\times 100 = 27.9%$.\n\n**Answer**: $MgCO_3$ = 72.1%, $BaCO_3$ = 27.9%."
