        "Bleaching powder was the first widely used chemical disinfectant for drinking water; its introduction in the 1850s (John Snow's cholera investigations) dramatically reduced waterborne diseases. Modern water treatment uses chlorine gas or sodium hypochlorite, but calcium hypochlorite tablets are still used for emergency disinfection and swimming pools.",
        "Plaster of Paris gets its name from large gypsum deposits near Paris (Montmartre). The $\\alpha$-hemihydrate form (dental stone) is made by autoclaving gypsum under steam pressure — it has higher density, strength, and lower water/powder ratio than the commercial $\\beta$-form.",
